WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.507 --> 00:00:02.090
SPEAKER: Today's big
data applications

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.063
tend to have a very
common data problem.

00:00:04.063 --> 00:00:05.480
While they all
have the tools they

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.490
need to manage volumes
of information,

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.890
they still lack the
ability to generate insight

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:10.940
from that data.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.210
And with traditional databases,
this is pretty tricky.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.640
Legacy relational database
management systems

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.410
are great at managing
lists of items,

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.630
but cumbersome for defining
how they relate to each other.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.690
Eventually, they realize
that the relationships

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.720
between data points matter
more than the individual points

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.680
themselves.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.380
This is where Neo4j comes in.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.810
It's a graph database,
meaning that relationships

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.210
between items are a
first class citizen,

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.640
allowing it to help you
generate valuable insights

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.490
about your data while
also remaining flexible

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.430
when expanding a data model or
conforming to changing business

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:44.080
needs.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.610
Graph is a core technology
that powers hundreds

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.430
of different use cases, from
real time recommendations

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.270
to fraud detection to network
and IT operations, master data

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.310
management, and more.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.740
On the technical
side, Neo4j fits along

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.870
with any microservices
you may develop,

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.750
can be used with Neo4j's
Bloom visualization,

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.690
or as a store for graph
QL-based applications,

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:09.200
analytics services, or any other
app deployed in a Kubernetes

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.860
easily.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.370
And here at Neo4j,
we love Kubernetes.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.680
We provide Docker containers,
allowing customers

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.650
to run in a clustered setup
for query scalability.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.290
But we found that customers
quickly ran into problems when

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.910
the containers proliferate.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.550
Thankfully, Kubernetes solves
the orchestration problem

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.140
with less work for you.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.930
Kubernetes has many advantages
for improving operations,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.450
but it can be challenging
for new users to set up.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.560
This is why Neo4j leverage
is Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.410
GKE is the best form of
managed Kubernetes that

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.600
allows our customers to
focus on the business

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.510
applications of their graph and
not be concerned with cluster

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.450
provisioning and maintenance.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.980
And with GCP marketplace,
you can go from zero

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.080
to a working clustered
graph database

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.700
in less than five minutes.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.640
Simply choose the Neo4j
solution, fill in some data,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.600
and get moving.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.570
It's a fast and easy way
for you to get started.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.490
GCP marketplace is
great, because it

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.250
gets you kickstarted with a
working setup tailored to best

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.160
practices quickly, while
still allowing power users

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.419
access to the
underlying resources,

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.830
so you can always customize
to your deployment needs.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.530
And it also lets users
track Neo4j deployments

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.800
as a single unified app in
our Kubernetes cluster instead

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.740
of just a bunch of
interrelated containers

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.590
Google Kubernetes Engine
combined with GCP marketplace

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.920
provide best in class
services for our Neo4j users

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.570
it lets you get up
and running fast

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.640
so you can focus on
your data and extracting

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.643
the value from that,
rather than all

00:02:34.643 --> 00:02:36.810
of the work of setting up
and managing your database

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.010
clusters.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.630
If you'd like to
know more, check out

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.520
the links in the
description below and feel

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.560
free to reach out with
any questions or comments

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.470
you may have.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.310
Thanks for watching.

